@page "/build/characters"

<PageTitle>Your characters — Prowlers &amp; Paragons</PageTitle>

@* Every character this browser or this account is holding, with the room to draw them all.

   **It is under `/build` rather than beside it, and that is one line of consequence.**
   `Areas.Of` reads the first path segment, so this address is `Area.Play` by construction — the
   step band and the budget strip come with it, the banner names the palette, and the switcher is
   in the banner where it is on every other builder page. A top-level `/characters` would have
   needed a case in `Areas.Of` saying "this one is the builder too", which is the special case the
   whole prefix scheme exists to avoid.

   **The step band draws with no step marked, and that is correct rather than tolerated.** Each
   step's `NavLink` is `NavLinkMatch.All`, so this address is none of them: what a reader sees is
   the six steps of the job they are in, with none of them claimed. The budget strip below it
   reports the character named in the panel's own first block, which is the character it is about —
   unlike the three areas the strip was pulled off, where the figure on screen belonged to somebody
   else.

   **The page is the panel and nothing else.** Every action on a character — open, read, discard,
   start another, import — is `CharacterManager`'s, and a second control here would be a second
   place to keep in step. The heading is what a page owes a reader that a panel inside one does
   not. *@
